### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification

The evidence refutes the claim's characterization of the intent behind the "Ballot Box" signs. On October 23, 2020, the [Union League Legacy Foundation](https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/election-2020-ballot-box-trash-can-ad-philadelphia-union-league-foundation-20201023.html) placed bright-red "BALLOT BOX" signs on two trash cans in downtown Philadelphia (at Broad/Sansom and 15th/Sansom Streets) to advertise its voting rights exhibit titled *"Ballot Box: America's Fight for the Vote."* Foundation chief administrative officer Kira Foley-Tuzman explicitly stated it was "certainly not intended" to mislead voters, calling it an "unfortunate advertising blunder." The signs were removed within hours of the foundation realizing the potential confusion, the District Attorney's Election Task Force confirmed no malicious intent, and the Streets Department even sorted through the trash checking for misplaced ballots—though none were found. No one was arrested or charged in connection with the incident. While the event itself occurred, the claim's assertion that the signs were *intended* to trick people into discarding their ballots is directly contradicted by the foundation's own public statement and reporting from the *Philadelphia Inquirer*.